SUMMARY = (
"Survey of 4D-printed shape-morphing classes with numbers read from fetched full texts (Europe PMC / NCBI PMC / publisher pages), plus checks of earlier agent files. "
"Headline results. (1) CONTRADICTION of spec-hygromorph-4d: a humidity-actuated vat-photopolymer part does exist. Goodarzi Hosseinabadi et al. (Small 2024, DOI 10.1002/smll.202306387) printed a PEG-polyurethane diacrylate ink by SLA (400 nm) and custom DLP (380 nm); thin films fold reversibly in humid air in under 1 s, a 50 um film folds over 5x faster than a 150 um film, and a 0.1 mm film lifts 50 mg (5x its own weight) at RH about 90 percent. It is a high-swelling (about 600 percent in water) hydrogel-like ink, not a Formlabs resin, with no curvature numbers and no Timoshenko analysis, so the 'no SLA/DLP humidity actuator' gap is closed but the 'Formlabs-resin hygromorph with a Timoshenko check' gap stays open. "
"(2) CONTRADICTION/REFINEMENT of the '<1 percent in 24 h' statement: it is true for Formlabs' 1 cm cube table (Elastic 50A V2 water 0.7 percent, Rigid 10K <0.1 percent; the 39.2 percent IPA figure for Elastic 50A V2 vs <0.1 percent for Rigid 10K is CONFIRMED) but Flexible 80A Resin V2's own datasheet (ASTM D471 slab) gives +8 percent mass and +3 percent volume after 1 day in deionized water (+9 and +7 percent at 1 week), and a Formlabs Dental LT Clear V2 part gained 29.53 ug/mm3 of water in a week in a third-party study (about 2.7 percent by mass if density is 1.1 g/cm3, my assumption). The number depends on the test method, specimen thickness and duration. "
"(3) Class numbers: thermal SMPs/LCEs respond in tens of seconds to minutes with large strains (LCE 80 percent programmed strain, 11 cycles; SMP bilayer about 20 s) but need heat; swelling hydrogels are slow (diffusion) and weak (forces under 1 N, about 2 kPa blocking stress vs 100-350 kPa for muscle), though thin films can be fast; cellulose-filled FFF bilayers reach 0.0155 mm^-1 in 30 min over 170 cycles (Cheng 2024); wood bilayers take 24 h at 4-8 mm and about 400 h at 15-30 mm. "
"(4) Timoshenko: published comparisons exist for wood, paper, FFF filaments and, newly, a 2026 FFF polyamide bilayer (predicted 36.4 vs measured 35.3 +/- 3.1 deg, n=3, arXiv 2604.26367); none found for vat-photopolymer or SLS parts. I re-ran the project's ceiling numerically (max f = 1.5000 on a 401x401 log grid) and applied it to a new vat-printed GelMA bilayer: ceiling radius 10.9 mm against a tightest measured 11 mm (plausibility only; figure data unreadable). "
"(5) Formal verification: no hygromorph or bilayer design with a proof-assistant-checked law was found (0 hits in Europe PMC and arXiv searches). The nearest item, arXiv 2604.26367, calls its pipeline 'verified' in a category-theory sense and its full text has no Lean/Coq/Isabelle mention. Searches were limited to Europe PMC, arXiv, Crossref and publisher pages, so absence is probable, not proven.")

LAY = (
"Some printed objects change shape on their own after they are made, for example a flat strip that curls when the air gets damp. "
"I checked the numbers for the main families: heat-triggered plastics, water-swelling gels, wood-fibre plastics and two-layer strips. "
"Heat-triggered plastics move in seconds to minutes but need a heat source; gels and wood respond to humidity alone but are slow and weak, like a wet sponge compared with a muscle. "
"The earlier team notes said nobody had made a humidity-driven part in a light-cured resin printer, but a 2024 paper did, using a special gel-like ink that folds a thin film in under a second when it senses moisture. "
"Formlabs' own tables say their ordinary resins soak up under 1 percent water in a day, which holds for small cubes, but one soft resin gains 8 percent weight in a day on a different test, so the true figure depends on how you test it. "
"I found no published design that was restricted by a computer-checked mathematical proof, which is the gap this project can fill.")

GLOSSARY = [
 {"term": "4D printing", "plain": "3D printing an object that then changes shape over time in response to heat, water or another trigger."},
 {"term": "shape-memory polymer (SMP)", "plain": "A plastic that can be fixed in one shape and springs back to another when warmed."},
 {"term": "liquid crystal elastomer (LCE)", "plain": "A rubbery plastic whose molecules line up, so it shrinks or stretches strongly when heated."},
 {"term": "hydrogel", "plain": "A soft, water-swollen gel such as jelly or a contact lens."},
 {"term": "hygromorph", "plain": "A material that bends or twists as it takes up or loses moisture from the air, like a pine cone scale."},
 {"term": "bilayer", "plain": "Two bonded layers where one expands more than the other, so the strip curls."},
 {"term": "Timoshenko formula", "plain": "A 1925 equation that predicts how much a two-layer strip curls from the layers' thicknesses, stiffnesses and the difference in how much each swells."},
 {"term": "ceiling of 3/2", "plain": "The project's proven limit: the strip's curvature times its thickness can never exceed 1.5 times the swelling difference."},
 {"term": "vat photopolymerisation (SLA/DLP)", "plain": "Printing by shining light into a tank of liquid resin so that it hardens layer by layer."},
 {"term": "SLS", "plain": "Printing by melting layers of plastic powder with a laser."},
 {"term": "FFF/FDM", "plain": "Printing by squeezing melted plastic filament through a nozzle."},
 {"term": "relative humidity (RH)", "plain": "How damp the air is, as a percentage of the most water it can hold."},
 {"term": "curvature", "plain": "How sharply something bends; 0.0155 per millimetre means it bends like a circle 64.5 mm in radius."},
 {"term": "work density", "plain": "How much useful work a material can do per unit of its volume, for example per cubic metre."},
 {"term": "diffusion", "plain": "Slow spreading of water through a material; thicker parts take much longer."},
 {"term": "isopropanol (IPA)", "plain": "Rubbing alcohol, used to wash printed resin parts."},
 {"term": "Lean kernel / machine-checked", "plain": "A proof program that checks every step of a mathematical argument so a human cannot slip an error past it."},
 {"term": "ASTM D471", "plain": "A standard soak test measuring how much a rubber gains in weight and volume in a liquid."},
]

